#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "d044f208-be6c-508f-b92d-221f36a8edcf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7352" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n\n- The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n\n- The number of months in a particular year" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105576 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53675175-8261-54ef-8dfb-2b3087b91239" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Probability Mass Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "528a4e6c-6338-56ae-a56b-3de96b69b32c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7353" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{2}{5},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; \\frac{4}{5}, \\mid x = 2g(x) = {\\frac{2}{5},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{10},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1h(x) = {\\frac{3}{10},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}x = 1; \\frac{1}{5}, \\mid x = 2$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $f(1)=-\\dfrac15$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $h$ do not sum to $1{:}$\n$\\dfrac25 + \\dfrac{3}{10} = \\dfrac{7}{10} \\neq 1$\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability distribution. Indeed, we have\n$0 \\leq \\dfrac{3}{10}, \\dfrac12, \\dfrac15 \\leq 1$\nand\n$\\dfrac{3}{10} + \\dfrac12 + \\dfrac15 = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22f7889-e5e4-5237-bb3b-fe1572ef4fbf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within an Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "f70939b9-5f06-57e8-ac4a-d4217a12aa6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7354" :question/problem "Compute $P\\left(X \\leq 3 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X=x)$ | $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ | $\\dfrac{3}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{10}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{5}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\le 3) &= P(x ∈ {1, 2, 3}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{3}{10} + \\frac{1}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35193ec5-030d-5c78-b536-08e23bd3b7d4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within a Set of Values"}] :question/id #uuid "c27e7286-1b80-532a-945e-e7897f4635eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-7355" :question/problem "Compute $P\\left(X\\in \\{2,4,6\\}\\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table above.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.2$ |" :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X ∈ {2, 4, 6}) &= P(X = 2) + P(X = 4) + P(X = 6) \\\\\n&= f(2) + f(4) + f(6) \\\\\n&= 0.1 + 0.1 + 0.2 \\\\\n&= 0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53675175-8261-54ef-8dfb-2b3087b91239" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Probability Mass Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3d7c216-268d-5d1b-83ce-33f94caf0741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105078} {:answer/id #uuid "bbeba875-1f14-5ccb-8dd5-d0a04ac7978a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105079} {:answer/id #uuid "e46625ee-a9fc-522c-8f12-9adc72f294a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$, $g$, and $h$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105080} {:answer/id #uuid "ee908b30-966f-5b75-b34b-dd09a51d6425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105081} {:answer/id #uuid "2b1fd3e3-923c-5b21-94a7-4436f71d20c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3d7c216-268d-5d1b-83ce-33f94caf0741" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105078} :answer-field/id #uuid "fec14c37-2555-5eff-bba4-071174236691" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105077}] :question/id #uuid "732d158b-710d-5722-b1ed-0167e4e3b912" :question/math-academy-id "q-83456" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are valid probability mass functions?\n\n$f(x) = {\\frac{1}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7g(x) = {\\frac{1}{3},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7h(x) = {\\frac{3}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x = 3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2},\\end{vmatrix}x = 7$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35193ec5-030d-5c78-b536-08e23bd3b7d4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Probability That a Discrete Random Variable Lies Within a Set of Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5858dbb-10f9-5bd9-8694-b6dfce860b63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105084} {:answer/id #uuid "064366ef-7d3f-5699-9915-2b598c22cb26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105085} {:answer/id #uuid "11146513-ee02-52e2-a190-e0fd1de320d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105086} {:answer/id #uuid "4676739b-d412-5a49-9318-b1f21577b915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105087} {:answer/id #uuid "49e19359-e41d-5a98-ac49-e62cc96b337a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11146513-ee02-52e2-a190-e0fd1de320d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105086} :answer-field/id #uuid "f03d2249-cafd-515b-91b9-adcc4fb91865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105083}] :question/id #uuid "54e9af2f-d595-580f-bd22-59bd51ee5360" :question/math-academy-id "q-83457" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X = x)$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ | $\\frac{4}{9}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n\nCompute $P(X ∈ {\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{3}{2}})$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability mass function shown in the table above." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
